"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[3427],{3427:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"forensic-medicine-test-2","slug":"forensic-medicine","subject":"Forensic Medicine","testNo":2,"durationMin":40,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A patient presents to the emergency department with a history of a gunshot wound sustained while on a hunting trip. X-ray findings are as given below. Which of the following weapons is most likely to produce this type of injury?","options":["Assault rifle","Machine gun","Shotgun","Sporting rifle"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Shotgun\\n\\nThe radiograph is suggestive of a close-range shotgun blast injury to the knee. Birdshot pellets are visible within and around the shattered patella, distal femur, and proximal tibia.\\n\\nThe ammunition for the shotgun is a cartridge made of a cardboard or plastic cylinder fitted into a metal base. The base carries a percussion detonator that is struck by the spring-loaded firing pin when the trigger is pulled.\xa0 The\xa0primer\xa0is located in the percussion cup and contains the detonator.\xa0 The cartridge contains a charge of propellant (gunpowder), above which are ‘wads’ or pistons of felt, and cardboard or plastic\xa0discs, or both. Above these is the charge of shot (pellets), which varies greatly in number and size, finally covered by a card or plastic disc.\\n\\nThe image given below shows the entry wound in the case of a shotgun. There is a central round hole, with multiple pellet holes scattered around the central hole.\xa0This wound is most likely to have been caused by a shotgun from a range of 1-5 m.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/cf2561cb34310c48287e.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f6c4befeb28dd10048a4.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/afda35abb75ce589a614.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/23caa9c3a44ed1813622.jpg"}]},{"n":2,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"The least common fingerprint pattern is _____________.","options":["Loops","Whorl","Arches","Composite"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Composite\\n\\nComposite type (1-2%) is the least common fingerprint pattern.\\n\\nPatterns of fingerprints:\\n\\n• Loops - Most common type (60-70%)\\n• Whorl - (25-30%)\\n• Arch - (6-7%) They may be plain, tented,\xa0or exceptional.\\n• Composite forms (1 – 2%) - least common\\n\\nThe study of ridge patterns in the skin is Dactylography/ Galton-Henry system/ Dermatoglyphics. Fingerprints are the impressions of patterns produced by the papillary or epidermal ridges present on the fingertips.\\n\\nClassification of fingerprints into different patterns is based on delta and core formation. Delta is a triangular arrangement formed where three separate ridge line flows come together. The core is the centermost portion of a ridge flow pattern.\\n\\nThe number of delta and core for each pattern is given below.\\n\\nPattern\\nDelta\\nCore\\n\\nLoop\\n1\\n1\\n\\nPlain arch\\n0\\n0\\n\\nTented arch\\n1 delta at the base of the upthrust\\n0\\n\\nWhorl\\n2\\n1\\n\\nThe image given below shows the delta and core in the different fingerprint patterns. For each type, the position of the core is marked with a green circle, and the delta is marked with a blue triangle.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/23c2d494ca2303b69f59.jpg"}]},{"n":3,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A 10-year-old child with a history of developmental delay is brought to a pediatric psychologist for evaluation after being involved in an incident where he caused significant damage to a neighbour\'s property. The child appears to lack an understanding of the consequences of his actions and exhibits signs of emotional immaturity. He will not be held guilty under which Bhartiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) section?","options":["24","23","21","22"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 21\\n\\nIn the given case of a 10-year-old child involved in property damage, he will not be held legally responsible under BNS (Bhartiya Nyaya Sanhita) Section 21 (IPC 83). This section specifies that a child aged 7 to 12, who demonstrates insufficient mental maturity, is not deemed criminally liable for their actions.\\n\\nGiven the child\'s developmental delays and lack of comprehension regarding the repercussions of his behaviour, he is protected from legal penalties or prosecution due to his inadequate mental maturity.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: BNS section 24(IPC 86) states that a person who commits an offence under voluntary intoxication is liable for the act.\\n\\nOption B: BNS section 23(IPC 85) states that a person who commits an offence under involuntary intoxication is not liable for the act.\\n\\nOption D: BNS section 22(IPC 84) states that an act of a person of an unsound mind (e.g. schizophrenia) is not liable for punishment or prosecution.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":4,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A man has been arrested and booked under Sec. 304-B IPC. Which of the following offenses has he been accused of?","options":["Death by rash/ negligent act","Dowry death","Culpable homicide","Grievous hurt"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Dowry death\\n\\nSection 304-B of the Indian Penal Code (I.P.C)/ Section 80 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) deals with dowry deaths.\\n\\nThis legal provision comes into effect when the untimely demise of a woman occurs by burns/bodily injury or any other cause apart from normal circumstances within the first 7 years of her marriage. Evidence of cruelty or harassment by her spouse or relatives of her spouse in connection with demands for dowry must be shown. An individual accused of dowry death (cognizable, non-bailable) is liable to imprisonment from 7 years to life.\\n\\nOther options -\\n\\nOption A -\xa0Death by rash or negligent act is punishable (imprisonment for up to 2 years with/without fine) by Sec. 304-A IPC.\\n\\nOption C -\xa0Culpable homicide is punishable by Sec. 299 IPC. It is an offense wherein the accused intentionally causes death or bodily injury likely to cause death. \xa0Culpable homicide is defined under section 100 of the BNS.\\n\\nOption D - \xa0Grievous hurt is punishable by Sec. 320 IPC (Sec.116 BNS). Grievous hurt refers to any hurt that endangers life, or which causes the victim to be in severe bodily pain, or unable to follow his ordinary pursuits for a period of twenty days. This includes emasculation, permanent privation of the sight of either eye, permanent privation of the hearing of either ear, the privation of any member or joint or the destruction or permanent impairment of the power of any member or joint, permanent disfiguration of the head or face, and fracture or dislocation of a bone or tooth.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":5,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A Forensic Examiner receives the following skull of a victim of mob violence. Which of the following conclusions he makes is correct:","options":["The victim could only have suffered from a gun-shot wound as indicated by Puppe’s rule","The victim could have suffered a blunt force trauma","It is not possible to determine the sequence of injuries from fracture pattern","Fracture pattern in skull injuries are random and offer no clue upon examination"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. The victim could have suffered a blunt force trauma\\n\\nPuppe’s Rule is true for all blunt injuries to the skull, and it is of particular importance in gunshot wounds. But the rule is not limited to gunshots.\\n\\nPuppe’s rule: It is possible to determine the sequence of injuries in skull fractures caused by blunt injuries to the head because the second fracture ends at the point where a break in cohesion is already present. A new fracture Line does not cross an existing fracture line which can be used to determine the sequence of injuries.\\n\\nEx:- Correct sequence of shots in this would be 1->2->3\\n\\nNote -\xa0Puppe\'s rule applies to any multiple blunt force trauma causing skull fractures.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/8597e25851a63ce6741e.jpg"]},{"n":6,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A dead body is found at a crime scene. On examining it, you notice that fixation of hypostasis has already occurred. What is the minimum time required for this finding to occur?","options":["4 hours","2 hours","6 hours","24 hours"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 6 hours\\n\\nFixation of hypostasis occurs 6 – 12 hours after death.\\n\\nPurple or reddish-purple discoloration of the skin due to collection of blood in toneless capillaries in dependant parts of the body. It is called postmortem hypostasis/ postmortem staining/ postmortem lividity/ livor mortis/ suggilation/ vibices.\\n\\nTiming of development of postmortem hypostasis:\\n\\n• Mottled patches – 1-3 hours following the death\\n• Uniform area of staining – 3-6 hours following death\\n• Fully developed and fixation of hypostasis – 6-12 hours","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":7,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"Crown heel length of a 6 month old fetus would be:","options":["36 cm","30 cm","12 cm","25 cm"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 30 cm\\n\\nCrown heel length of a 6-month old fetus would be 30 cm.\\n\\nAccording to the Rule of Hasse:\\n\\n• During the first five months of pregnancy the square root of the length gives the approx. age of the fetus in months.\\n• During last five months the length in centimeters is divided by five gives the age in months.\\n\\nAge of fetus in first five months = square root of the crown heel length in cm.\\n\\nAge of the fetus during last 5 months of pregnancy = (Crown heel length in cm)/5\\n\\nSo, crown heel length = age in months \xd7 5 i.e. 6 \xd7 5 = 30 cm.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":8,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"Olive Green colored urine is seen in poisoning with:","options":["Kerosene","Oxalic acid","Organophosphates","Carbolic acid"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Carbolic acid\\n\\nOlive green urine is seen in carbolic acid poisoning.\\n\\nIn carbolic acid poisoning if the patient survives for 48 hrs there will be carboluria followed by anuria. The victim will pass dark, smoky urine which soon turns olive green on standing due to oxidation of hydroquinone and pyrocatechol.\\n\\nCarbolic acid or phenol is an organic acid that is used as a disinfectant and solvent. It has a\xa0characteristic phenolic odor. Poisoning by carbolic acid is called carbolism.\\n\\nOn exposure to the skin, carbolic acid coagulates the proteins and causes the formation of an eschar that will come off as slough later. It can damage the nerves and cause tingling and numbness.\\n\\nOn ingestion, a burning sensation may be felt, along with nausea and vomiting.\xa0The acid can also cause pulmonary and laryngeal edema, leading to difficulty breathing, and ultimately respiratory failure.\\n\\nDiagnosis of carbolic acid poisoning can be made by adding a few drops of 10% ferric chloride to the patient\'s urine sample. If the sample turns violet or blue, it indicates the presence of phenolic compounds. The urine sample would also reduce Benedict\'s and Fehling\'s solutions.\\n\\nTreatment involves\xa0removing contaminated clothing and washing the skin. If the patient has\xa0ingested the poison, cautious gastric lavage with\xa020% alcohol, glycerol, or vegetable oil should be carried out.\xa0MgSO4 or liquid paraffin should be left in the stomach to remove surface phenol and prevent deep penetration. Parenteral saline can also be administered to correct the circulatory depression, dilute the carbolic acid content of the blood, and promote its excretion. Hemodialysis can be carried out if there is renal failure.\\n\\nPost mortem, the viscera should be preserved in a saturated salt solution. The skin has a reddish appearance with necrosis or ulceration. The body has a characteristic phenolic odor. The esophagus may look reddish and like parchment. The internal surface of the stomach may show\xa0prominent rugae with a leathery appearance,\xa0due to the opaque, coagulated, grey, or brown mucosa.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/eb6f4778a3e4fc81ddd3.png"}]},{"n":9,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A man was found dead due to starvation.Which of the following findings will be seen on autopsy?","options":["Distended gall bladder","Expanded lungs","Enlarged liver","Hypertrophied heart"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Distended gall bladder\\n\\nIn starvation, the gall bladder is distended.\\n\\nIn starvation,\xa0bile does not get secreted into the duodenum due to the absence of food entering the stomach and intestine. So bile gets accumulated in the gall bladder and gets distended.\\n\\nStarvation:\\n\\n• The feeling of intense hunger lasts for 30–48 hours.\\n• A person can survive for 10–12 days without food and water and for 6–8 weeks without food alone.\\n• Loss of 40% of body weight is usually fatal.\\n• Loss of 70–90% of body fat and 20% of body protein is fatal.\\n• There is a\xa0reduction in the weight and size of all organs except the brain.\\n\\nOther features of starvation:\\n\\n• Dry, lustreless hair\\n• Brittle nails\\n• Pale skin with edema around ankles\\n• Loss of fat in subcutaneous tissue, in the omentum\\n(sparing of fat in female breast and orbit till later)\\n• Atrophy of muscles and darkening due to lipochrome\\n• The heart is atrophied with empty chambers\\n• Pale and collapsed lung\\n• Shrunken liver with necrosis\\n• Tissue paper-like intestinal mucosa\\n• Empty bladder","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":10,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"Which of the following tests on live birth are based on the changes in the middle ear?","options":["Wredin’s test","Fodere’s test","Ploucquet’s test","Breslau’s second life test"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Wredin’s test\\n\\nWredin’s test is based on the changes in the middle ear.\\n\\nTests for live birth:\\n\\n• Wredin’s test\xa0– Before birth, the middle ear does not contain air. It only contains gelatinous embryonic connective tissue. With respiration, the eustachian tube opens and the gelatinous substance is replaced by air. For this, the middle ear must be opened underwater by removing tegmen tympani. If an air bubble comes out, the test is said to be positive. This is not at all reliable.\\n• Fodere’s test\xa0– Based on the increase in the weight of the lung due to increased blood flow after birth. The weight of\xa0unrespired lung is 30–40g whereas the weight of\xa0respired lung is 60–66 g.\\n• Ploucquet’s test\xa0– Based on the ratio of the weight of the lung to the weight of the body in unrespired and respired lungs.\\n\\n• Unrespired lungs:\xa0Weight of lungs: weight of body = 1:70\\n• Respired lungs:\xa0Weight of lungs: weight of body = 1:35\\n\\n• Breslau’s second life test:\xa0Air enters the\xa0stomach and intestine during respiration in a live-born fetus. So if respiration has taken place, they float in water, otherwise, they sink.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":11,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A female convict was allegedly tortured and raped by an investigating officer while in police custody. Under which section will the inquest be conducted?\\n\\nBNS - Bhartiya Nyaya Sanhita","options":["BNS Section 63 (375 IPC )","BNS Section 68 (376-C IPC)","BNSS Section 196 (176 CrPC)","BNSS Section 194 (174 CrpC)"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. BNSS Section 196 (176 CrPC)\\n\\nThe given scenario is indicative of custodial rape. A magistrate inquest is done under BNSS Section 196 (Section 176 CrPC).\\n\\nCustodial rape is defined as the offence committed by persons who are in supervisory positions indulging in sexual intercourse with women in their custody by inducing or seducing the woman by taking advantage of his official position.\\n\\nBNSS 196 (Section 176 CrPC) deals with magistrate inquest in which the cause of death is not under natural circumstances. Magistrate inquest may be conducted by the District Magistrate, Sub-Divisional Magistrate, Tahsildar, or any other Executive Magistrate empowered by the State Government.\\n\\nIndications for Magistrate Inquest:\\n\\n• Custodial rape\\n• Death in police custody or police interrogation\\n• Death in police firing\\n• Death in prison, reformatories, Borstal school\\n• Death in a psychiatric hospital\\n• Dowry death\\n• Cases of exhumation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A- BNS Section 63 (Section 375 IPC)deals with the definition of rape. In BNS 63, the Age of Consent of 15 years is replaced by 18 years in BNS. Exception 2 of Section 63 states that “sexual intercourse or acts by a man with his wife, the wife not being under 18 years of age, is not rape”.\\n\\nOption B- BNS Section 68 (Section 376 IPC) deals with any person who holds authority or a fiduciary role, is a public servant, manages a jail, remand home, custody facility, or women’s/children’s institution, and misuses their position or relationship of trust to induce or seduce a woman in their custody, charge, or present on the premises into having sexual intercourse with them (not amounting to rape), shall face imprisonment for a term of no less than five years, which may extend to ten years, along with a fine.\\n\\nOption D- BNSS Section 194 (Section 174 CrPC) deals with the police inquest. It is done in suicide, homicide, killing by any animal or machinery or accident, and death under circumstances that raise suspicion of another person committing the offence.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":12,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"In Breslau’s second life test, the organ tested is _______","options":["Brain","Stomach and Intestine","Lung","Heart"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Stomach and Intestine\\n\\nIn Breslau’s second life test the organ tested is Stomach and intestine.\\n\\nBRESLAU’S SECOND LIFE TEST:\\n\\n• It is based on air entering stomach and intestine during respiration in a live born foetus.\\n• Stomach and intestine are removed by tying double ligatures at each end and placed in water.\\n• Inference:\\n• A floating viscera giving out air-bubbles when opened under water is positive test and suggestive of live birth.\\n• False positive – in case of stillbirth, air might enter the stomach when child tries to free obstruction of air passages.\\n• Mucus, saliva and air bubbles in stomach – breathing has occurred.\\n• Only mucus – breathing has not occurred.\\n• Blood, meconium, liquor amnii in stomach – child alive at or shortly before birth.\\n• Milk in stomach – child was alive for sometime after birth.\\n• Test is invalid in case of putrefaction.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":13,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"Which type of mechanical asphyxia does the image shown below represent?","options":["Burking","Bansdola","Mugging","Garroting"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Mugging\\n\\nThe type of mechanical asphyxia shown in the image is mugging.\\n\\nMugging is a type of strangulation caused by holding the neck of the victim\xa0in the crook of the elbow. Pressure is then exerted either on the front of the larynx or at one or both sides by the forearm and upper arm. In mugging, the attack is\xa0usually made from behind. The image below shows strangulation by mugging.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Burking is a type of homicidal asphyxial death comprising of smothering and traumatic asphyxia. In burking, the assaulter throws the victim to the ground and kneels or sits on the chest of the victim, preventing chest movements. He/she then covers the nose and mouth of the victim with his/her hands.\\n\\nOption B: In bansdola, one strong bamboo or stick is placed across the back of the neck and another across the front. The victim is squeezed to death by tying both ends of the stick with rope. Sometimes, two sticks are used across the back and front of the upper part of the chest and squeezed forcibly. This causes laceration of the muscles, and rib fractures and interferes with respiration.\\n\\nOn examination, a bruise can be seen in the center of the neck across the windpipe, corresponding to the width of the stick or the substance used. A similar mark can be seen on the back of the neck if two sticks are used.\\n\\nOption D: Garrotting\xa0involves attacking the victim from\xa0behind\xa0using a\xa0ligature. The ligature is thrown around the unaware victim’s neck and then tightened by twisting it with a lever such as a rod or stick within the ligature. Earlier in Spain, garroting was an official method of judicial execution.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/4f1aa54d1aec63f95245.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/7ff262ff9f922fd3a181.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/795c62ee9000b2b0b02e.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1e0f98b2e942a4c589dc.jpg"}]},{"n":14,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A patient presents to you with a history of ingestion of one uncrushed seed similar to that shown in the image below. He is asymptomatic at presentation. Which of the following can be anticipated in this case?","options":["Opisthotonos followed by respiratory paralysis","Confusion followed by coma","Severe convulsions followed by death","No poisonous action"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. No poisonous action\\n\\nThe image shows seeds of Strychnos-nux vomica. If swallowed uncrushed, the seeds of nux vomica have no poisonous action, as they are not dissolved in the gastrointestinal tract, and are passed entirely in the faeces.\\n\\nStrychnine is a competitive antagonist of glycine at the postsynaptic receptors in the motor neurons of the anterior horn of the spinal cord. This leads to increased activity in the motor neurons. The fatal dose is 15-30 mg of strychnine or one crushed seed.\\n\\nPatients typically present with symptoms similar to tetanus such as\\n\\n• Convulsions are clonic initially but eventually become tonic. The anti-gravity muscles experience the most severe convulsions, causing the body to typically arch in hyperextension—a position called opisthotonus.\\n• Risus sardonicus which results from the contraction of the jaws and facial muscles in which the corners of the mouth are drawn back.\\n• If the spasm involves the abdominal muscles, the body bends forward (emprosthotonos), or to the side (pleurosthotonus).\\n• If death is preceded by a severe convulsion, as in strychnine poisoning, it causes an increase in the body temperature known as postmortem caloricity.\\n\\nThe first step in the management of strychnine poisoning is to control convulsions with benzodiazepines or barbiturates and the patients should be placed in a dark room to avoid triggering further convulsions. Activated charcoal can be used to adsorb unabsorbed poison after a thorough stomach wash. Acidification of urine can be done to increase the excretion of strychnine.\\n\\nThe toxin strychnine does not affect any receptors in the brain and the person remains conscious till death. Strychnine poisoning is characterized by conscious seizures.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/66ceca5ea7492999b2dc.jpg"]},{"n":15,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"All of the following tests can be used to check the cessation of circulation, except:","options":["Magnus’s test","Winslow’s test","Diaphanous test","Icard’s test"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Winslow’s test\\n\\nWinslow’s test is used to check the cessation of respiration while the Diaphanous test, Magnus’s test and Icard’s test are done to check the cessation of circulation.\\n\\nWinslow\'s test consists of placing a bowl of water on the chest and monitoring the movement on the water\'s surface.\\n\\nOther tests for confirming cessation of breathing. are:\\n\\n• No audible breath sounds on continuous auscultation of the upper part of the chest and in front of or on the larynx for a minimum of 5 minutes\\n• Feather test – No movement of a feather held in front of the nose\\n• Mirror test – A mirror held in front of the nose does not turn dim due to any moisture in breath.\\n\\nTests for confirming cessation of circulation:\\n\\n• No audible heart sounds on continuous auscultation on the precordial area of the chest for a minimum of 5 minutes and a flat ECG recording for 5 minutes\\n• Magnus’s test (Ligature test) - Fingers fail to show congestion distal to a ligature applied at their base\\n• Diaphanous test (Transillumination test) - Failure to show redness in the webspace between the fingers on transillumination from behind\\n• Icard’s test - Fluorescent dye on being injected at a given site in a dead body fails to produce any discoloration\\n• Fingernail test - No blanching and filling of blood in the fingernail on the application of pressure and release","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":16,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"The venom of the snake shown in the image is most likely to be:","options":["Haemotoxic","Myotoxic","Neurotoxic","Vasculotoxic"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Myotoxic\\n\\nThe snake in the given image is a sea snake\xa0and it\'s venom is\xa0myotoxic.\\n\\nSea snakes are myotoxic. Myotoxin causes\xa0a non-enzymatic mechanism that leads to severe muscle necrosis.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/8c23578723f9ff2e67d4.jpg"]},{"n":17,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A 35-year-old man files a case against a surgeon who left a cotton swab inside his abdomen after a hernia repair. Which of the following best describes this scenario of medical negligence?","options":["Res judicata","Res ipsa loquitur","Res indicata","Novus actus interveniens"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Res ipsa loquitur\\n\\nRes ipsa loquitur\xa0best describes this scenario under medical negligence.\\n\\nRes ipsa loquitur means the facts of the case speak\xa0for\xa0themselves.\\n\\nNormally, in cases of medical negligence, the burden of proof lies on the patient to prove the doctor guilty. However, the patient need not prove any negligence under Res ipsa loquitur.\\n\\nThe conditions to be fulfilled for Res Ipsa Loquitur are:\\n\\n• The injury or damage would not have occurred without negligence\\n• The doctor is in exclusive control of the instrumentation or circumstances\\n• The patient must not contribute to his own injury or damage\\n\\nSeveral situations can be used as defenses by the doctor against medical negligence. This includes no duty owed to the plaintiff/ patient, the doctor\'s duty has been discharged according to prevailing standards, a scenario of misadventure\xa0that\xa0may be therapeutic, diagnostic, and experimental, or an error of judgment.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Res judicata under section 300 Cr. P.C states that if a question of negligence against a doctor has already been decided by a court in a dispute between the doctor and his patient, the patient will not be allowed to contest the same question.\\n\\nOption C: Limitation\xa0or\xa0Res indicata\xa0indicates that a suit for damages for negligence should be filed against the doctor within two years from the date of alleged negligence. A suit\xa0filed after two years will be dismissed. But, where a breach of duty to provide care as per a particular contract between a patient and a doctor is committed, legal action can be initiated up to three years from the date of alleged negligence.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Novus Actus Interveniens\xa0is a situation where the cause of disability or death passes from the doctor to a later negligent action of some other person. It means a person is responsible not only for his actions but also for the logical consequences of those actions.\xa0For a plea of Novus actus, an element of negligence is absolutely essential.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":18,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"Identify the type of skull fracture in the given image.","options":["Pond fracture","Hinge fracture","Comminuted fracture","Depressed fracture"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Pond fracture\\n\\nThe given image shows a pond fracture of the skull.\\n\\nIt appears as an indentation or a buckling-in of the skull, without actual fracture. It is more commonly seen in infants due to the elasticity of the bones. They are caused by an obstetric forceps blade or a blow from a blunt object. The dura mater is not torn and usually, the brain is not damaged.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/b3f8940ef3acb9301630.jpg"]},{"n":19,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"The ‘en masse’ removal of organs is a feature of which of the following autopsy techniques?","options":["Letulle technique","Virchow technique","Ghon technique","Rokitansky technique"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Letulle technique\\n\\nThe ‘en masse’ removal of organs\xa0is done in the Letulle technique.\\n\\nIt is the removal of the cervical, thoracic, abdominal and pelvic organs as a single organ mass. They are subsequently dissected into organ blocks.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":20,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A deceased male was posted for a post-mortem examination. His relatives reported that he had a tattoo over his skin, which was not found on examination. Which of the following structures would you examine?","options":["Arteries","Skin","Liver","Lymph nodes"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Lymph nodes\\n\\nThe lymph nodes\xa0should be examined in this case.\\n\\nSome amount of material or dye used for creating tattoo marks is transported through lymph to the regional lymph nodes.\xa0Hence in cases where a tattoo mark has disappeared\xa0naturally or has been removed, the lymph nodes must be examined before making any conclusions.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":21,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A 5-year-old boy is brought to OPD with complaints of pain over the forearm. On detailed examination, several wounds with cigarettes burns are found. X-ray showed a spiral fracture of the radius. Which of the following is least likely seen in this condition?","options":["Periosteal shearing","Subdural hematoma","Multiple injuries at same stage of healing","Soft tissue swelling"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Multiple injuries at same stage of healing\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0battered baby syndrome. The classical feature of this condition is injuries inflicted are of different ages and in different stages of healing. This is due to the repetitive nature of child abuse.\\n\\nA battered child is one who has received repetitive physical injuries as a result of non-accidental\xa0violence, produced by a parent or guardian, there may also be deprivation of nutrition, care, and affection.\\n\\nThis syndrome must be considered in any child:\\n\\n• In whom the degree and type of injury varies with the history given\\n• When injuries are of different ages and in different stages of healing\\n• When there is a purposeful delay in seeking medical attention despite serious injury\\n• Who exhibits evidence of fracture of any bone, subdural hematoma, failure to thrive, soft tissue swelling or skin bruising\\n• Who dies suddenly\\n\\nSkeletal survey, which includes the following, should be done in a case of suspected child abuse:\\n\\n• AP views of the entire skeleton\\n• Dedicated views of hands and feet\\n• Lateral views of the appendicular skeleton, skull, and spine","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":22,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"You were posted for an autopsy of a 34-year-old tribal man who was under police custody for interrogation. On external examination, signs of third-degree torture were present. Which of the following incisions is preferred to detect and evaluate the hidden subcutaneous hemorrhage?","options":["Modified Y-shaped incision","Elongated X-shaped incision","I-shaped incision","Y-shaped incision"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Elongated X-shaped incision\\n\\nThe clinical scenario is suggestive of custodial death. The incision that is preferred to detect and evaluate the hidden subcutaneous hemorrhage is the\xa0elongated X- shaped incision.\\n\\nAn elongated X-shaped incision is used to dissect subcutaneous structures in the back of the body. It helps identify and evaluate the extent of blunt injuries when superficial imprints are faint. This type of incision is helpful in the reconstruction of events before death and is practiced in custodial death.\\n\\nThe image below shows the \'elongated X- shaped\' incision:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/74418ed1d7d3f14ce091.jpg"}]},{"n":23,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A forensic pathologist is examining a body discovered buried in soil, noting the decomposition stage suggests the body had been buried for approximately 8 days. If the body had been left exposed to air under similar environmental conditions, what would be the estimated time since death based on Casper\'s dictum?","options":["32 days","64 days","1 day","8 days"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1 day\\n\\nAs the decomposition stage suggests the body had been buried for approximately 8 days,\xa0the equivalent decomposition in air would have taken place in 1/8th of that time.\\n\\nCasper\'s rule or dictum states that a body decomposes in the air twice as fast as submerged in water, and eight times faster as buried in earth.\\n\\nTherefore, the rate of decomposition is Air > Water > Soil. It is helpful in the estimation of time since death.\\n\\nFree access of air hastens putrefaction, partly because the air conveys organisms to the body. Putrefaction is rapid in a body buried in a damp, marshy or shallow grave without clothes or coffin, because the body is exposed to constant changes of temperature. Putrefaction is more rapid in warm, fresh water than in cold, salt water. It is more rapid in stagnant water than in running water.\\n\\nNote: Forensic taphonomy is the study and interpretation of postmortem processes of human remains i.e. the history of a body following death.\\nThe ratio of the time a body takes to decompose in air, water, and soil is 1:2:8. This means that the time taken by soil is greater than water and air. So, the correct order of time taken for decomposition is Soil > Water > Air.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":24,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"A 19-year-old boy is brought to the doctor because his family found him dressed as a woman. He says that he derives sexual pleasure from this act. However, he has no wish to undergo sex-change surgery. What is this type of perversion called?","options":["Transsexualism","Fetishistic transvestism","Frotteurism","Gender dysphoria"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Fetishistic transvestism\\n\\nThe given scenario is suggestive of fetishistic transvestism.\\n\\nIt\xa0is a\xa0paraphilia characterized by recurrent sexually arousing fantasies, sexual urges, or behavior involving the wearing of clothes of the opposite sex and appearing as a member of the opposite sex. It is also called\xa0transvestic fetishism. It is usually seen in males who obtain sexual pleasure by wearing female clothes.\\n\\nFetishistic transvestism is a paraphilia whereas transvestism is a gender identity disorder.\xa0There is no hormonal/genital abnormality in fetishistic transvestism.\\n\\nOption A: Transsexualism refers to\xa0an obsessional desire to become a member of the opposite sex and seek surgery for anatomical alterations.\\n\\nOption C: Frotteurism\xa0refers to a\xa0person who obtains sexual gratification by contact with another person (rubbing his private parts against female buttocks, breasts, or thighs, in public places). It is punishable under\xa0Section 290 IPC, with fine up to 200 Rs.\\n\\nOption D: Gender dysphoria is a term that\xa0describes a sense of unease that a person has due to\xa0a mismatch between their biological sex and their gender identity. This sense of unease or dissatisfaction can lead to depression and anxiety and can have a harmful impact on daily life.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":25,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"What is the active principle of the poison shown in the image?","options":["Ricin","Abrin","Thevetin","Bhilawanol"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Bhilawanol\\n\\nThe image shows black, heart-shaped seeds with hard rinds that identify them as seeds of Semecarpus anacardium, and its active principles are bhilawanol and semicarpol. Common names are marking nut, bhilwan, bibva, bhela, and oriental cashew.\\n\\nWithin the fruit is a thick fleshy pericarp that yields the active principle, which is a brown oily resinous fluid called bhilawanol.\\n\\nThe juice when applied locally to the skin produces irritation, painful blisters, itching, and eczematous eruption. The seeds are used to produce an artificial bruise. Symptoms such as pain, fever, and painful passage of brownish urine may be seen.\\n\\nA large dose taken orally can produce blisters in the mouth and throat, with gastroenteritis. It also produces dyspnea, cyanosis, tachycardia, coma, and death. The fatal period is 12 to 24 hours.\\n\\nMedicolegal importance is the use of the juice by malingerers to fabricate wounds. Instilled in the eyes it can cause conjunctivitis or ophthalmia due to its local irritant effect.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Ricin is the active principle of Ricinus communis. It is a toxalbumin or phytotoxin that inhibits protein synthesis by affecting the ribosomal RNA polymerase. The seeds are flat to oval-shaped and have dark brown markings (shown below). It is a chemical warfare agent.\\n\\nOption B: Abrin is the active principle of Abrus precatorius. The seeds are egg-shaped, scarlet in color, and have a black spot at one end (shown below). On ingestion, it causes gastroenteritis with bloody diarrhea, rectal bleeding, tachycardia, and circulatory collapse. Injecting the active substance can mimic viperine snakebite. Rarely, it may lead to tetanic convulsions.\\n\\nThe seeds of A. precatorius are decocted and mixed with datura, opium, onion, spirit, and water to form a paste. From this, small sharp needles called suis are made. They are used for hunting animals and even for homicide purposes.\\n\\nOption C: Thevetin is a cardiac poison derived from the plant Cerebra Thevetia (yellow oleander). All parts of the plant are poisonous. Symptoms of intoxication are characterized by vomiting, diarrhoea, headache, dizziness, mydriasis, and loss of muscular power. Cardiovascular signs include a weak and irregular pulse rate with hypotension. Possible complications are heart block and cardiorespiratory failure.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/e7cbfb7a003c2efdbcff.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/7b8012c363b928e91d39.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/745e01652102e592e963.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/aa8e78f74f7c39353f08.jpg"}]},{"n":26,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"Kevorkian sign is seen in:","options":["Cornea","Vitreous humour","Pupil","Retinal vessels"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Retinal vessels\\n\\nKevorkian sign is a postmortem change that occurs in the retinal vessels.\\n\\n• It is characterised by fragmentation of blood column in the retinal vessels.\\n• It appears within minutes after death and lasts for about 1 hour.\\n• It can be visualised by using an ophthalmoscope.\\n\\nOther postmortem changes occuring in the eye\\n\\n• Tache noir: One of the post-mortem ocular signs. A film of cell debris and mucus forms two yellow triangles on sclera on either side of cornea, with base towards the margin of cornea and apex towards medial or lateral canthus of eye. It appears within 3-4 hours of death.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/007b9352bd85c3a4cc54.jpg"}]},{"n":27,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"Which of the following indicates a female skull?","options":["Prominent mastoid process","Large parietal eminences","Square orbits with round margins","Small frontal eminences"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Large parietal eminences\\n\\nLarge parietal eminences indicate a female skull.\\n\\nDifferentiating features between male and female skulls:\\n\\nTrait\\nMale\\nFemale\\n\\nGeneral size\\nLarge\\nSmall\\n\\nArchitecture\\nRugged\\nSmooth\\n\\nSupraorbital ridges\\nProminent\\nLess prominent\\n\\nMastoid process\\nProminent\\nLess prominent\\n\\nOccipital region\\nMarked muscle markings\\nNo marked muscle markings\\n\\nFrontal eminences\\nSmall\\nLarge\\n\\nParietal eminences\\nSmall\\nLarge\\n\\nOrbits\\nSquare with round margins\\nRound with sharp margins\\n\\nMandible\\nLarge, broad ramus\\nSmall, small ramus\\n\\nChin shape\\nU-shaped\\nV-shaped","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":28,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"Which of the following acts as a physiological antidote to scorpion venom?","options":["Atropine","Physostigmine","Norepinephrine","Prazosin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Prazosin\\n\\nPrazosin acts as a physiological antidote for scorpion venom.\\n\\nThe venom is a clear, colorless, proteinous toxalbumen, having a hemolytic and neurotoxic effect. Its toxicity is more than that of snakes, but only a small quantity is injected. The venom is a potent autonomic stimulator, resulting in the release of massive amounts of catecholamines from the adrenals.\\n\\nIf the scorpion has hemolytic venom, the reaction is mainly local and simulates the viper snake bite. The scorpion sting will have only one hole in the center of the reddened area. The extremity will have edema, pain, and reddening. This usually lasts for one to two hours.\\n\\nThe symptoms produced by a neurotoxic venom are similar to a cobra bite. There is usually no marked reaction in the local area. Nausea, vomiting, extreme restlessness, fever, various types of paralysis, cardiac arrhythmias, convulsions, coma, respiratory depression, and death may occur within hours from pulmonary edema or cardiac failure.\\n\\nPrazosin\xa0can counteract the alpha-adrenergic effects of autonomic stimulation, such as hypertension.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":29,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"You are declaring a patient brain-dead by using the Harvard criteria. All of the following parameters are checked for, except:","options":["Isoelectric ECG waves","No spontaneous muscular movements","Loss of elicitable reflexes","Unresponsiveness to painful stimuli"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Isoelectric ECG waves\\n\\nIsoelectric EEG is included in Harvard criteria for brain death and not ECG.\\n\\nHarvard criteria for brain death include:\\n\\n• Unreceptivity and unresponsiveness,\xa0demonstrated by an absent motor response to pain in all extremities.\\n• No movements\\n• Apnea- impaired brainstem activity indicates damage to the medullary cardio-respiratory centers. This manifests as a loss of centrally controlled breathing.\\n• Absence of elicitable reflexes\\n\\n• Cranial nerve II, III (optic and oculomotor)- absent direct and consensual light reflex. This is associated with dilated pupils measuring about 9 mm.\\n• Cranial nerve VIII (vestibulocochlear)- absent oculocephalic (Doll\'s eye phenomenon) and oculovestibular (unresponsive to caloric testing) reflexes.\\n• Cranial nerve V, VII (trigeminal and facial)- absent corneal and jaw reflexes.\\n• Cranial nerve IX, X (glossopharyngeal and vagus)- absent cough and gag reflexes.\\n\\n• Isoelectric EEG (confirmatory)\\n\\nAll these tests should be recorded. If there is no change after 24 hours, then the patient is declared brain dead.\\n\\nPhiladelphia protocol, Minnesota criteria are other criteria used to diagnose brain death.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":30,"subject":"Forensic Medicine","text":"In the civil negligence case against a doctor who bears the onus of proof?","options":["Police not under rank of sub inspector","Patient","Doctor","Magistrate"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Patient\\n\\nIn the case of civil negligence, the burden of proof is on the patient.\\n\\nThe question of civil negligence arises when a suit is filed in a civil court:\\n\\n• By a patient or his/her relatives against the doctor for getting compensation from the doctor if he/she has suffered an injury due to the doctor\'s negligence.\\n• By a doctor against the patient or his/her relatives to collect the fees, the payment of which has been refused due to alleged professional negligence by the doctor.\\n\\nIn either case, the patient has to prove that the doctor has committed professional negligence.","img":false,"explImg":false}]}')}}]);